MZ77 benchmarks and profiling
=============================

Tools for measuring the LZ77 codec (src/lz77.c, src/bitio.c).  The GUI
is not measured.  Nothing here is built by 'make' or run by 'make check'.

Targets, run from the top of the build tree:

  make corpus         download and unpack the Silesia corpus into
                      bench/corpus/silesia (about 68 MB, once)
  make bench          time the codec as configured on the whole corpus
  make bench-compare  build the codec with several compiler settings and
                      time each one on the whole corpus
  make profile        profile one input with gprof, perf and valgrind

Results go to bench/results/COMMIT/ (COMMIT-dirty when src/ or bench/
has uncommitted changes), so runs before and after a change sit side by
side.  Every run also writes machine.txt: CPU, governor, kernel and
compiler.

Corpus
------

bench/corpus/generated/ is made by 'mz77-bench --generate' from a fixed
seed, 20 MB per file:

  text.dat    English-like words: typical text
  random.dat  uniform random bytes: incompressible, worst case
  zeros.dat   all zero bytes: longest matches, best case
  mixed.dat   a short phrase with 1 byte in 8 random

bench/corpus/silesia/ is the Silesia corpus (Sebastian Deorowicz, 2003),
12 files and 211 MB of real data (text, executables, databases, images,
XML), the usual benchmark for general-purpose compressors.

The driver
----------

bench/mz77-bench calls lz77_compress and lz77_decompress directly.  It
runs each file --repeat times (default 5) and reports the median wall
time from CLOCK_MONOTONIC, the throughput in MB/s (10^6 bytes) of
uncompressed data, and the ratio archive size / input size (lower is
better).  The first run of each file checks that decompression gives
back the input, so a faster but broken codec fails loudly.  Temporary
archives go to /dev/shm, so the disk does not add noise.

  bench/mz77-bench --mode=compress --repeat=3 FILE...
  bench/mz77-bench --csv=out.csv --label=try1 FILE...

make bench: bench.csv, bench.txt
--------------------------------

One row per corpus file with the flags ./configure chose.  Configure
with --enable-release for numbers that match the shipped program.

make bench-compare: variants.csv, variants.txt
----------------------------------------------

Compiles src/lz77.c, src/bitio.c and bench/bench.c directly with each of

  O0, O1, O2, O3                machine-independent optimisation levels
  O3-native                     -march=native: machine-dependent
  O3-native-lto                 plus link-time optimisation
  O3-native-lto-pgo             plus profile-guided optimisation, trained
                                on test/input and a small synthetic set,
                                not on the measured corpus

variants.csv has one row per variant and file; variants.txt totals each
variant over the corpus.  The list is at the top of bench/compare.sh.

make profile: profile/
----------------------

Profiles one input: PROFILE_INPUT if set, else silesia/dickens (10 MB of
English text), else generated/text.dat.

  gprof.txt                  flat profile and call graph, over 5 runs.
                             Built with -O2 -pg -fno-inline, so the small
                             static helpers (byte_at, hash3, ht_insert)
                             appear as functions of their own.  Call
                             counts are exact; times are 10 ms samples.
                             Time inside libc (memmove, fputc, fgetc) is
                             not instrumented and is listed as _init.
  perf-stat-compress.txt     hardware counters for compression alone and
  perf-stat-decompress.txt   decompression alone, mean of 5 runs: cycles,
                             instructions (IPC), branch misses, cache
                             misses, L1 data cache misses
  perf-report.txt            sampled hot functions with call stacks
  perf-lines.txt             sampled hot source lines (file:line)
  perf-annotate-*.txt        hot instructions inside each codec function
                             (perf 6.12 sometimes crashes here; a function
                             it crashes on gets no file)
  callgrind.txt              exact instruction counts per function
  callgrind-lines.txt        the same per source line of lz77.c and bitio.c
  cachegrind.txt             simulated cache and branch-predictor misses
  summary.txt                the headline numbers from all of the above

perf needs kernel.perf_event_paranoid <= 2 (the Debian default is 2);
the scripts count user-space events only, so no root is needed.

callgrind and cachegrind run the program about 50 times slower but give
the same counts on every run, which makes them the best way to compare
before and after a change.  Use wall time and perf for real speed.

Getting stable timings
----------------------

Laptop CPUs change clock speed with temperature and power.  Plug the
machine in, close heavy programs, and compare runs made in the same
conditions.  Increase REPEAT for steadier medians:

  make bench REPEAT=9
